{
  "index_test": "whole-animal-in-vivo",
  "index_test_evidence": "Recently, there has been renewed interest in comparative oncology &#x2013; the study of naturally developing cancers in animals as models for human disease &#x2013; as one way to improve cancer drug development and reduce attrition of investigational agents. Tumors that spontaneously develop in pet dogs and other companion animals as a result of normal aging share many characteristics with human cancers, such as histological appearance, tumor genetics, biological behavior, molecular targets, and therapeutic response.",
  "decision": "exclude",
  "reason": "The study discusses the potential of using naturally occurring tumors in pets as models for human disease but does not report any quantitative measure of agreement or concordance between animal and human results. It is a workshop summary discussing the rationale and potential for integration, not a study reporting such a statistic."
}
